{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CggUyP4kBxuyLvaV8Gyyd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Assignment.** For tetrahedral d⁷, the ground state is ⁴A₂(F). The three spin-allowed bands are:\n- ν₁ = ⁴T₂(F) ← ⁴A₂\n- ν₂ = ⁴T₁(F) ← ⁴A₂\n- ν₃ = ⁴T₁(P) ← ⁴A₂\n\n**Dq.** In Td, ν₁ equals 10Dq exactly, so Dq = 4070/10 = 407 cm⁻¹.\n\n**B.** I used the ⁴T₁ F–P configuration-interaction matrix with energies measured from the ⁴F barycentre. The ⁴A₂ ground state lies at −12Dq. The matrix is:\n\n| | ⁴T₁(F) | ⁴T₁(P) |\n|---|---|---|\n| ⁴T₁(F) | 6Dq | 6B |\n| ⁴T₁(P) | 6B | 15B |\n\nEach band energy is ν = E(T₁) + 12Dq. With Dq = 407, 12Dq = 4884 cm⁻¹ and 6Dq = 2442 cm⁻¹. For ν₃, E = 16400 − 4884 = 11516 cm⁻¹. The secular equation is:\n\n(2442 − 11516)(15B − 11516) = 36B²\n\nThis gives B ≈ 650 cm⁻¹. That is about 0.67 of the free-ion value of roughly 970 cm⁻¹, which is a sensible nephelauxetic reduction.\n\n**Consistency check.** The data are not fully self-consistent in this simple model.\n- With Dq = 407, the ν₂ equation has no real solution for B.\n- Fitting ν₂ and ν₃ alone gives Dq ≈ 484 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 594 cm⁻¹. That Dq predicts ν₁ ≈ 4840 cm⁻¹, not the observed 4070 cm⁻¹.\n- The mismatch is probably due to spin–orbit coupling, which is large for Co(II) and mixes states of the ⁴T₁ and ²E, ²T₁ manifolds. The ν₂ band is also often broad, so its assignment is uncertain.\n\nI therefore take Dq from ν₁ and B from ν₃, and treat both as approximate: Dq ≈ 407 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 650 cm⁻¹. C cannot be determined, because no spin-forbidden bands are given.\n\n{\"Dq\": 407, \"B\": 650, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq = ν1/10 and B from the ⁴T1(P) F–P matrix; ν2 is not fully consistent, so the values are approximate, and C is not determinable from spin-allowed bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":795